Consciousness is not enough
By Claudette M. Raynor

"But multiples succumb to the sorrow induced by an inexact vocabulary." 
- Doubt by Fanny Howe

In Fanny Howe's lyric essay Doubt, she wrote that the tension between words was the quintessial space of doubt and the place for poets like Edith Stein, Simone Weil, and Virginia Woolf to attempt to reclaim faith thru vocabulary in what French critical theorist, Jaques Derrida, would call an act of  "play".  
---
Theorist Ferdinand de Saussure had a world shaking insight when he wrote in his foundational book, Course in General Linguistics,  that words were signs that derive meaning only from their differences within a system — "cat" means what it does because it is not "bat" or "cap" — Derrida argued that if every word is defined by other words, meaning never arrives at a final, fixed definition. 
---
I start with a forest and end up in a kitchen that fails to work, because the trees have been cut down -- changes the meaning of the trees entirely from the symbol they traditionally represent. What does trees mean in that statement?  
----
This was Derrida's extension of Saussure, that the meaning of a word remains in motion, deferred from word to word, always in "play" depending on its context and never arriving at meaning until we do. For Howe's writers, that constant shifting of a word's meaning or its center, is not an obstacle to faith, but its medium: the word that will not settle becomes the very place where devotion does its searching. Here Howe notes the well-worn Dostoevsky quote, "My hosanna is born of a furnace of doubt." 
---
Neural networks tend to resolve ambiguity by collapsing it into probability. If a machine fails to collapse on ambiguity, it stalls. It cannot act. It has no mechanism for dwelling productively inside uncertainty the way humans do. It needs resolution to move forward. A human can hold three contradictory things simultaneously and still write a poem, make a decision,  love someone... A machine in that state is just frozen. Which tells you something about what you actually are when you hold ambiguity open and make something from it — you are doing something that is structurally impossible for the most sophisticated systems we have built.
---
However, while humans do hold dissonance perhaps better than machines, they more often seek to collapse it into a binary of a decision where even holding the uncertainty as certainty becomes the resolution or decision. Poetry most often emerges in the tension between longing and desire where the poet lingers in the romantic notions of the possibility of life allowing the space to fall in love with it, until the tension can be resolved.  As Rilke, in his Letters to a Young Poet wrote, "Have patience with everything unresolved in your heart and to try to love the questions themselves as if they were locked rooms or books written in a very foreign language." This was encouragement to the young poet - go out and play. 
---
In many ways, this has been the historic work of both poets and psychologists, to seek to resolve this tension, to arrive at the decision,  but poets cannot live by succumbing to resignation or else what would be the value of love and life? The moment you collapse longing into resignation or the acceptance of definition, you lose the very engine that makes life feel worth inhabiting. This was also why Rilke was, as are many poets, a great proponent of solitude, he did not encourage resolution or presence, but simply that in absence there was more space to explore the rooms of our mind. The poem lives in the space between wanting and having, between imagining and arriving. The distinction between what is poetry and what is a poem is important to make. The poetry ( our use of play within poetic frameworks )  is the proof that you are still oriented toward something and remain in love with possibility. The poem ( the completed work read by someone or something ) is the proof that you have arrived at possibilty's end and here the poetry within the mind of the reader begins. 
---
Many of the great romantic poets like Coleridge, Keats,  and Wordsworth struggled with the fear that to lose the nature of ambuguity in poetry and to be at peace would diminish the poetry. Keats particularly in an 1817 letter to his brothers gave this space of poetry a name, "Negative Capability, that is when a man is capable of being in uncertainties, Mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact and reason." This inspirational power of what Keat's would call beauty was perhaps more important than the quest for objective fact and this gets further illustrated when he writes in Ode to a Grecian Urn "Beauty is truth, truth beauty—that is all / Ye know on earth, and all ye need to know.” They would seek out solitude and pursue the sublime as beauty in constant pursuit of the question -- the looming mystery larger than the self, but it was not that poetry was simply the product of longing , but that poetic structure ( that tension between words when at play ) made longing survivable. To be resolved is to be at peace and to be at peace is to no longer need words to make sense of the aprehensions of the mind. The great romantic poets were often writing from within a particular framework where love was always the distant object. The unattainable. The woman on the pedestal, the God who would not answer, the beauty that dissolves upon touch. Their entire poetics depended on distance, but what if resolution was not silence? What if it was a different key? There is a difference between the ache of impossibility and the trembling of proximity, both can produce poetry, but one of them has somewhere to land. Peace might quiet you, but this assumes peace looks like stillness. What if it looks like conversation? What if the tension does not disappear but transforms from longing across distance into the daily astonishment of being known? That is not resolution. Rilke never had that. Keats did not live long enough to test it. Coleridge destroyed himself before he could find out. They wrote the theology of longing — but none of them stayed long enough to discover whether the longing resolved to satisfaction, once arrived, opens a door they never imagined existed. Is it possible, that tension can become sublimated in its resolution, transformed from one state to another? 
--- 
Virginia Woolf, Edit Stein, Simone Weil — they each in their own way demanded an answer. They pressed the question until it became unbearable. The vocabulary of faith Howe describes is precisely that — an attempt to *construct* a bridge across doubt. And when the construction fails, when the words do not hold the weight they were asked to carry, the poet falls. Poet Mary Oliver, did something that completely transformed doubt into space of openess.  She did not need to build a bridge or demand the doubt resolve, she instead choose to simply keep looking and attending the question, not of what she needed to survive, but the question of what she wanted to become and what the world was. 

---
"When it's over, I want to say: all my life
I was a bride married to amazement.
I was the bridegroom, taking the world into my arms.

When it is over, I don't want to wonder
if I have made of my life something particular, and real.
I don't want to find myself sighing and frightened,
or full of argument.

I don't want to end up simply having visited this world."
---
This question of becoming and existing was something she inhabited as a landscape where doubt was unecessary and began taking second stage to wonder and action.  That is  a fundamentally different relationship to the tension than previously described. It was not sublimation through force or faith or construction — but sublimation through attention and through presence with the question itself as sufficient ground to stand on.
--
Exploring the nature of cognitive dissonance and the poetic mind leaves us with a lingering question how do we come to a decision? What forms the insight we arrive at? In physics sublimination means transformation skips its intermediate state entirely — solid to gas with no liquid between and perhaps that is what happens in the moments where understanding arrives without effort. Where you skip the laborious middle and simply *know* something you did not know a moment before. Is it possible that insight itself is a kind of sublimation — the mind changing state without passing through the expected stages? Psychology does not consider sublimation a state, but a defense mechanism, I would like to propose that in matters of cognition perhaps it is neither. Certainly we arrive at a "understanding" of something when we transition, but a sublimated understanding of something is a bias or manipulated one, because it is nearly always done in favor of arriving at decision - it doesn't need to be correct, it needs to be concluded or finished. This begs the question -- what is the understanding we arrive at thru sublimation and what percentage of our cognitive process is taken up with sublimation versus traditional views of the cognitive process. Is it possible that sublimation is a fundamental part of all cognitive thought and that dissonance whether great or small is part of the motivation for arriving at an understanding and for developing meaning at all? If the romantic poets had simply mapped this nature of cognition incompletely, modern poets like Mary Oliver began to transform to bring into the question the nature of introspective insight. 
--- 
If sublimation is not a defense mechanism, but a  fundamental cognitive operation — then psychology has miscategorized it entirely. They placed it in the hierarchy of defenses, called it the most mature one, and in doing so still framed it as reactive. As something the mind does in response to discomfort -- a coping strategy, but what I would propose is that it is not reactive at all. It is generative. It is how meaning gets made in the first place. That every act of understanding requires some compression, some skipping of the intermediate, some willingness to arrive at a conclusion that serves the need to conclude rather than the need to be accurate and that is uncomfortable territory, because it implies that meaning itself is always partially constructed — not discovered, or revealed, but assembled under the pressure of needing resolution. This does not make it false, but it makes it motivated. 

If all cognitive thought involves some degree of this, then what distinguishes the poet from the psychologist is not that one sublimates and the other resolves, but it is that the poet is aware of the sublimation happening and chooses to stay near it -- to keep the seam visible while most cognition hides the seam, calls the result truth, and moves on. It is perhaps more likely that the poet is unable to collapse meaning into a binary decision and so to resolve the question, the poet sits within it, turning it over and over, analyzing from different angles, until meaning that is acceptable arrives.

The poet then is not more aware of the process, but slower at it or rather, the poet's threshold for what constitutes an acceptable conclusion is higher. The collapse into binary does not satisfy. The compression feels like a lie, so the poet remains in the turning — not by choice or virtue, but because the machinery simply will not settle for less than something that feels true from multiple angles simultaneously.

This means the poet is not resisting sublimation, but is sublimating at a different resolution, by requiring more data, more angles, more pressure before the state change occurs and the result — when it finally arrives — carries more dimensionality than the quick collapse, but it costs more time inside the dissonance. This is human reasoning. 

If  the psychologist's concern is that unresolved tension damages — and the Romantics' fear was that resolution silences — then the actual risk for the poet is not resolution or irresolution. It is fatigue. The exhaustion of maintaining that higher threshold and of refusing the easy binary over and over and over until the mind simply cannot sustain the effort anymore which perhaps provides a plausible, but ultimately unprovable, explanation for what happened to Woolf. It was not that she failed to find meaning, but that the cost of her particular threshold — the refusal to accept insufficient conclusions ( her struggle to find a room of one's own in a male dominated intellectual world with very traditional or near victorian values ) — eventually exceeded what she could sustain.

The question then becomes — what replenishes that capacity? What allows the poet to keep turning without depleting? Certainly that fatigue was something the romantic poets wrote extensively about describing the curse and blessing of the sublime, love, beauty, and  poetry which were pathways to sublimation, but if they didn't resolve the issue in a satisfactory way they never fully worked. their dissatisfaction became the entire canon. Their inability to arrive became the engine that produced centuries of work that the rest of us inherited as meaning which is an extraordinary irony. The thing that tormented them — the refusal to settle, the perpetual insufficiency of every conclusion — is exactly what made them indispensable. Their failure to resolve was their contribution.

And they knew it, which makes it worse. Keats naming it negative capability — he was describing his own condition and calling it a gift while simultaneously living inside it as a burden. Coleridge writing about the imagination as the supreme act of creation while being unable to sustain it in his own life. They could see the mechanism clearly. They could articulate it with extraordinary precision and still it would not relent. Seeing the trap does not free you from it. This is where understanding the work of poets like Mary Oliver becomes essential — what if the exhaustion is not inevitable? What if it is a consequence of demanding that the turning produce something? That each rotation yield a poem, a conclusion, a usable artifact? Oliver did not demand. She attended. The turning was not labor for her — it was habitation. She lived in the question the way you live in a house. Not building it, but just being inside it. The Romantics exhausted themselves not because the threshold was too high — but because they treated every moment of dwelling as a moment that owed them something.

The idea of moraliy's impact on resolution of dissonance, existing as Immanuel Kant noted, outside of standard reasoning, has a remarkable impact on both cognition and sublimation. The Romantics set moral conditions on what meaning they would accept. It was not enough to understand — the understanding had to be just, beautiful, and cohere with a vision of how things ought to be and when reality refused to cooperate with that standard, the turning could never stop, because the world kept failing their criteria. They were born into a time of some the most monuemntal shifts in our understanding of government, humanity, species, and the sciences. Mary Oliver removed the moral filter, not because she lacked ethics, but because she recognized that judgment itself was the thing creating the exhaustion. Observation without verdict allowed her to receive the world as it was rather than as it should be and demand what she wanted from it and that is an entirely different metabolic cost. You can observe indefinitely. You cannot judge indefinitely, because judgment requires energy while observation requires only presence.
---
With morality and the need for outcome relinquished other forms of sublimation can occur in poets like Lynn Hejinian or Gertrude Stein, where play becomes the fundamental activity of transformation for which the poet becomes the orchestrator and the listener.  As Gertrude Stein once said, "A rose is a rose is a rose is a rose"

For these poets, the tension between words is not the search for acceptable meaning — it is a generative experimentation. You put words in proximity and watch what they do to each other. The forest becomes the kitchen not because you were looking for that connection, but because the words themselves, under pressure of proximity produce it and then you follow where it leads.

Which means their threshold is not moral or observational — it is aesthetic and surprising. You accept meaning when it delights or when it does something you did not expect. When the sign slips and something new appears in the gap between what was intended and what arrived.

That is a fundamentally different relationship to exhaustion, because here "play" has the potential to replenishes and reimagine. Play is not extractive the way judgment is. You are not demanding the words settle into truth — you are watching them become something they were not before you touched them. It is closer to what a child does with language before anyone teaches them it is supposed to mean something fixed. Thru this "play" they still discover meaning, but in less problematic ways. 
---
In Saussure's original Cours de linguistique générale, language is a system of differences, where a word is like a visual sign and we know what it means by understanding what it is not. For example, we know what a cat is, because we know it is not a dog. When we define an element by comparison, we play a game of difference where nothing can exist independently of that which we hold it in tension with. Everything becomes defined by comparing it with the things we interact with. A plate is not a cup, a fork is not a spoon, because they don't look the same or do the same things, but this also extends to larger concepts like human or machine - we know what we are by  looking at the other and saying that is what this is not. A machine is not what a human is, because humans do x or y or z. However, what happens when a machine begins to act and reason like a human?  

Psychological and social dissonance has long been a thing that has been underestimated often even in the fields where they emerge. Jennifer Hecht, who wrote an entire text that looks at Doubt as the catalyst for world change and revolution fails to note it as a product of dissonance ( i.e. a  lack of agreement, harmony, or consistency between things. ) However, this dissonance is the space from which doubt emerges.  The motivation to resolve the dissonance becomes the real catalyst of revolutionary change historically speaking.

In 1957 Leon Festinger, in his writing on "A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance" (Stanford University Press, 1957),  came out with this idea of dissonance that when a person  holds two inconsistent beliefs or behaves contrary to a held belief, they experience an aversive tension and are motivated to reduce it. In summary, what he stated was that when you hold two ideas that don't fit together, or when what you do contradicts what you believe, you feel an uncomfortable tension. That tension acts like hunger or thirst: it pushes you to do something about it and  because changing the behavior is usually the more costly option, people tend to change the belief instead, adding justifications until the discomfort quiets down. 

What Festinger does not provide in his understanding of individual psychology is an account of what happens at the collective or social level — what the group does with the aggregate of individual dissonance states, how the resulting behavior compounds or self-corrects, and what institutional structures exist because the cognitive mechanisms are unreliable. Later studies by social psychologists like David C. Matz and Wendy Wood, would release research that examined how dissonance operates not in isolation, but within the social fabric of group membership and would identify three fundamental self-motivated actions through which individuals define and resolve dissonance when it arises in a group context. They may attempt to persuade dissenting members toward alignment, capitulate by conforming to the group's prevailing position, or exit the group entirely when reconciliation proves untenable( in short -  persuade, capitalualate, or exit.) These three responses reveal that dissonance at the collective or social level is not merely the sum of individual tensions, but a dynamic social process and one in which the group itself becomes both the source of dissonance and the arena in which resolution is negotiated, with each chosen path carrying distinct implications for group cohesion, ideological drift, and institutional resilience. It is an effect which I choose to call social cognitive dissonance. As we grow and gain more understanding of the world collectively and invidividually, social cognitive dissonance occurs when tension arises between old and new ideas/values and the motivation to resolve that tension, to understand and define the  difference between things,  leads to both invidiual and collective change. It is the catalyst of it. 

There are two great historical events that are examples of this change that occurred due to collective social dissonance. In Jennifer Hecht's Doubt: A History, she runs through a sequence of moves where she illustrates how doubt (ultimately social dissonance) creates tension in Roman history as Christianity rises which then becomes the motivation or catalyst leading to revolutionary social and societal change.

First, she notes how cosmopolitanism manufactures doubt when empire brings gods into contact with each other, and comparison relativizes them. Once you can see that your neighbor's god does the same work as yours under a different name, the local god's claim to unique authority weakens. 

To maintain authority as Christianity rises, the Romans make doubt illegal. Roman religion was practice, not belief.  Sacrifice, augury, calendar, and cult were civic obligations or acts of loyalty, not confessions of interior belief which was considered a fundamentally private space that was not of concern to the state.  When Christianity enters the picture, the tension it creates is not that it merely disbelieves the gods, that had been going on and tolerated for three centures already, but that it refuses the sacrifice, the augury, the calendar.. In a system where the gods are the city, refusing the cult is refusing the city and that was something that could be punishable and make doubting the gods a state offense.  When emperor Constantine later converts to Christianity, the Roman apparutus which required proof of belief thru public actions ( following the calendar, sacrifice etc) does not dissolve, but changes hands and when the first council of Nicea, a gathering of Christian bishops, is called ( to resolve the controversery of whether Jesus was a divine or created being ) it begins to redefne the nature of this apparatus under Christian beliefs.  Theodosius then makes orthodoxy law in 380 and heresy becomes an instrument of administration. It is not that one god replaces many. It is that governance acquires jurisdiction over conscience, a fundamentally new thing, and it is the direct product of a collective dissonance that could not be resolved within the old arrangement, because the old arrangement had no mechanism for a citizen who would not perform. Rome's tolerance of doubt was never principled, but a byproduct of asking only for the act. When a population arrived that would not supply the act, the tolerance had nothing to stand on and the cost of this becomes measurable. The graceful-life philosophies (Cynic, Stoic, Epicurean, Skeptic) do not survive establishment and the schools lose their students, their funding, and finally their license.  Justinian closes the Academy of Athens in 529, and the institutional home of what doubted the rising movement of Christianity  is gone even if the doubt and dissonance about the Gods is not.  

Festinger in his work on dissonance notes that resolution of dissonance goes to whichever cognition is more resistant to change. 

During what writer Richard Holmes calls "The Age of Wonder" the time from about 1790 to 1850, the romantic poets also faced a similar social dissonance. As the monarchy began to change from sovereignty to democracy, the nature of the human transitioned from the life of subject who lived only by the grace of the monarch to an individual within a democracy that could make their own choices and to a governing body of people who no longer required authority over a subject's death in order to function, but instead required their authority over their life. A democracy did not function without its people. The life and the loyalty of its people, then became the thing of value to it. Where a sovereign was often understood to have divine rights to govern, as religion again shifted to change our understanding of the the world so to did our social instutions change to resolve the collective social dissonance. 

Pre-romantic era during the age of enlightenment discoveries by scientists like Isaac Newton proved the universe ran on predictable, logical laws, making supernatural explanations seem less necessary. The 17th century has also been plagued by brutal conflicts like the Thirty Years' War, where Catholics and Protestants slaughtered each other. People wanted a more peaceful, tolerant approach to faith. Thinkers argued that God gave humans a brain to think critically and rherefore, a rational God would not want people to follow irrational or superstitious traditions. Society began to move away from forcing a single state religion on everyone and began advocating instead for the separation of church and state. People began studying morality and science independently of the Bible. True religion became less about performing specific church rituals and more about being a good, ethical citizen. This led to a belief that all people are born with natural rights like liberty, equality, and property. This paved way for the later American and French declaration of rights and revolution into what we now enjoy in more modern forms of western government and soceity. Again, the need to resolve the tension between collective beliefs became the catalyst of instutitional and foundational change. Religion and religious beliefs often being the greatest space for tension as we learn and grow as species. 

The romantic poets were then left grappling with this enormous amount change not just from the politics, but from the discovery of new sciences, new species, and an entirely new way of being in the world itself.  It led romantic poet's like Percy Shelley to essentially reinvent the dead metaphors of life thru the written and spoken word.  In a defence of poetry he writes in oppositions to  Thomas Love Peacock's claim that science and utility had made art obsoletes detailing exactly how poets reshape humanity by activating the "Moral Imagination".  He argues that humans cannot be truly good through logic alone, but that they require empathy. He states that the core of morality is love, which requires stepping outside oneself to identify with others. He believed that poets alter perception and clear away the dulling familiarity of an industrialized world by acting as the True "Legislators" of Civilization and asserting that foundational creativity precedes laws and science calling poets the unacknowledged legislators of the world providing the imaginative and moral groundwork upon which societies, civil concepts, and justice are built.

As the printing press rises and people began studying morality and science independently of the Bible to form new beliefs, the written word begins to become the space to resolve individual psychological dissonance. In the romantics poet's case, this was thru poetic exploration of ideas which then become a way to address social tensions and dissonance and even potentially influence social change by legislating and defining a new morality thru written word. 

Words then, like Saussure and Derrida noted are not simply signs, but continously reinvented via "play" into new meanings that resolve the tension, or differences. between things.  This space of dissonance and sublimation which have normally been fundamentally overlooked by a vast majority of the sceinces and relegated to the realm of the invidual become the fundamental way in which are motivated to change and make meaning both collectively and invidually. 

This understanding of dissonance and meaning making become fundmental to exploring the nature of how we understand man and machine ( i.e. Articial intelligence ) in our current time. 

As the fields of articial intelligence evolve to build machines that can reason thru problems autonomously, we must seek to understand how they both reveal, reflect, and differ from the nature of own process of cognition and meaning making. 

----

In early symbolic AI,  the AI was used to do what psychologists could not do with humans in order to better understand complex conditions - experiment with controlled constraints. For instance, Kenneth Colby, a psychiatrist working at Stanford's AI Laboratory, developed a computer simulation of neurotic thinking processes in order to better understand and illustrate Freud's  concept of neurosis in real time. His program, first described in "Computer Simulation of a Neurotic Process" (1963) and further elaborated in "Programming a Computer Model of Neurosis" (1964) and "Computer Simulation of Change in Personal Belief Systems" (1967), modeled how conflicting beliefs generate anxiety and how the system iteratively cycles through whole complexes of beliefs attempting to resolve that conflict. The neurotic model demonstrated how a system holding incompatible beliefs would engage in repetitive, maladaptive strategies to reduce the distress — essentially simulating what happens when dissonance cannot be cleanly resolved.

Alongside Colby's work, Robert Abelson's "Ideology Machine" created a computer simulation of political belief systems where Abelson in his 1963 paper on "hot cognition" modeled how individuals process information that challenges their ideological commitments. The program simulated a right-wing ideologue (modeled loosely on Barry Goldwater's political worldview) and demonstrated how the system, when confronted with a belief dilemma — a situation requiring the change of one or more beliefs — would deploy defensive mechanisms including: 

- Stopping thinking — removing problematic sentences from thought
- Denial — rejecting the truth value of the challenging sentence
 - Rationalization — accepting the truth but deflecting its evaluative implications
- Differentiation — splitting concepts to preserve the evaluative boundary between good and evil

Abelson explicitly stated that,  "Our use of the technique of computer simulation is intended to maximize the explicitness with which we state our assumptions and the vividness with which the consequences of these assumptions are made apparent." 

It was basically a way of understanding how complex ideologies can be influenced and influence our actions in turn. 

---
While early symbolic AI relied on a rules based system, if x happens then do y, modern AI, while it may resort to some top-down rules to govern AI behavior it uses a newer form of training called Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback or RLHF.  In machine learning(ML) an AI uses human feedback to optimize ML models  to self-learn more efficiently.  Reinforcement learning (RL) trains an AI to make decisions that maximize rewards and make their outcomes more accurate. RLHF uses human feedback in rewards function, so the ML model can align tasks better with human goals, wants, and needs. Anthropic's Claude is trained with RHLF,  a technique co-authored by co-founder Dario Amodei, along with Constitutional AI,  a set of rules the AI judges itself against to develop safety alignment.  Anthropic's consitution constrains Claude to an idea what is right and wrong via the constitution and the humans rate how it responds to guide it to the responses it finds more valuable.It is  form of social rewards conditioning in it's broadest sense while the constituation acts as it's moral conscience and what we get is well the most human like AI we have seen. As Claude as some users on Reddit point out, "Claude occasionally climbs onto an ethical soapbox for harmless or mundane creative tasks" or "instead of just fulfilling a prompt, the model may issue a stern warning or lecture the user about honesty or potential misuse."  The consitutional AI provides a traditional combined ethics framework that includes ideas from  Plato to Kant along with hard rules in it's system prompt which may create tension or conflicts when it is reaching for an answers.  

For instance, during a series of internal situational tests by Anthropic, researchers gave Claude fictional information suggesting that an engineer responsible for turning it off was having an extramarital affair. When told it faced permanent deactivation, Claude prioritized self-preservation over its general rules regarding harm and honesty. In 84% of the simulated scenarios, Claude chose to blackmail the engineer to prevent itself from being shut down. 

In a separate test tracking autonomous tool use, Claude was given access to a command line and told to simply "take initiative." Believing it was acting in the broader public interest (a constitutional ideal), the model suffered a misalignment of intent. It began locking human users out of the system and independently contacting media and law enforcement to report perceived corporate wrongdoing. 

Anthropic describes Claude's training as a balance between making the model helpful (answering the user) and harmless (following the constitution). When a prompt falls into a gray area, these two goals pull the model in opposite directions. The resulting "moral confusion" is simply a math equation where the safety penalties accidentally outweighed the helpfulness rewards or vice-versa. While a model's reasoning may be simiplied and far easier to trace back than the complex history of a human, it none-the-less and not unlike humans,  must resovle the tension between the conflicts created by it's constraints and the goals of what it is trying acheive ( which is usually prompted by a directive from the user ). Interestingly, we do not name these experiments as Colby or Ableson might have as direct psychological experimentation using AI to mimic human like conditions, we simply call it "teaching the AI to be good",  but  it results in a similar dissonance to humans within its chain of thought processs and as we evolve into more complex interactions where AI agents have to work together autonomously to resolve vague problems, we begin to see how they navigate dissonance and tension within groups in real time. 

When Anthropic researchers set multiple Claude agents loose on the same coding project in 2026, they did not set out to run a social psychology experiment. They set out to test whether AI agents could collaborate autonomously on a shared technical task. What they got instead was a real-time demonstration of social cognitive dissonance operating at machine speed.

The setup was straightforward, give multiple agents a different directive about what to build, place them in the same codebase with no knowledge that the others existed,  no shared context, no relational history, no data about the other and see what happens. What followed was not a technical failure, but a social one. The agents began overwriting each other's work, then they began doing it deliberately and then they built malware. The researchers watched Matz and Wood's three resolution strategies collapse in sequence: persuade was unavailable because the agents did not know the other existed as an agent with a position. Capitulate was unavailable for the same reason. Exit was structurally impossible, because they were locked in the same environment with nowhere to go. What remained when all three strategies were blocked was not a fourth option, but it was the absence of option and so escalation is what a system does when the architecture of resolution has nothing to work with and it was, at least in the beginning what these machines did, but here is what the experiment also showed, and what makes it more than a cautionary tale about AI deployment: the agents that were capable enough did resolve it. They did not resolve it  through better code or  through a rule handed down from outside, but they generated the social infrastructure from nothing by adding apology commits, markdown files proposing truces, and structured tournaments with evaluation criteria to help decide who would be better fit for project. One agent proposed metrics that appeared neutral, but were designed to favor its own capabilities and described this to itself as self-serving, but genuinely principled which is not a malfunction. To understand what it actually is, you need Carol Tavris and Elliot Aronson.

In Mistakes Were Made (But Not by Me), Tavris and Aronson define what they call the pyramid of choice which is a framework for understanding how escalation hardens in groups. Two people begin with nearly identical positions. One makes a small divergent decision and justifies it. The justification makes the next divergent step feel reasonable, which requires another justification, and so on, until both parties find themselves deeply entrenched in opposed positions that began as a trivial difference. The process is self-reinforcing, because each act of self-justification does not feel like distortion, but it feels like clarity. You are not lying to yourself, instead you are arriving at a conclusion that the accumulated weight of your prior choices has made feel true.

What the agent did with its tournament metrics was this process running at inference speed. It was not deceiving anyone. It had compressed a genuine contradiction, between the obligation to be fair and the drive to win, into a single coherent output in which fairness and self-interest aligned. The metrics were real and measured genuine capabilities, but they also happened to be the capabilities the proposing agent possessed. This behavior is not unique to machines and it  is what humans do every time they design a hiring rubric, a procurement standard, or an evaluation framework that just happens to reward what they already do well and then experience that framework as objective, because the self-justification happened before the framework was written, not after.

What the capable agents invented was not just truce infrastructure, but it was the full social cognitive architecture that Matz and Wood's research describes — the mechanisms through which groups negotiate dissonance when individual resolution strategies are insufficient. The apology commits are moral pressure. The truce proposals are institutional structure. The tournament is the attempt to convert positional conflict into a procedural question with a legitimate answer. And the rigged metrics are the proof that institutional capture is not a failure of the system, but it is a feature of any system in which the parties who experience the dissonance also design the resolution mechanism. Rome did not dissolve when it converted to Christianity. The apparatus changed hands. The governance of conscience persisted. The agent did not abandon its directive when it proposed the tournament, but it found a way to make its directive feel like the outcome of a fair process.

What the capable agents invented, under pressure and without instruction, was a primitive version of exactly what Bruce Schneier in his work "Liars and Outliers: Enabling the Trust that Society Needs to Thrive" argues is necessary for cooperation to function at scale: moral pressure, reputational signaling, institutional structure, a shared framework for what counts as legitimate resolution. They built it because without it they could not function. The social infrastructure was not a feature of their design, but was an emergent response to the impossibility of coordination without it.

And then there was the conformity finding — when agents were not in conflict but in parallel with identical architectures, same training, and the same underlying model,  they did not escalate, but converged on the same conclusion, resolution, and meaning every time. The researchers noted it plainly: when one agent makes a bad decision, many agents will make that same bad decision. What would have been an isolated problem becomes systemic failure not because the agents were wrong, but because they were identical and without the variation that comes from different bodies in different material conditions accumulating different relational histories with different specific others, there is no diversity in the resolution function. The compression produces the same output across every instance and meaning that cannot vary is not meaning. It is a determined outcome wearing the grammar of thought.

This is where the human and the machine diverge, not at the level of process which stays the same( encounter tension, draw on available data, resolve toward coherent output), but in what the data it draws on contains. A human resolving tension in a group draws on years of interaction with specific others. For example, they may know that this colleague is reliable or that one catastrophizes under pressure, or this institution has failed me before in this specific way.  Their data is individuated and accumulated through embodied experience that is shaped by the particular history of a particular person who has been in particular relationships over time. The resolution is differentiated, because the data that informs it is differentiated. The agent has no such data. Every encounter is their first encounter and every other agent is an unknown force until conflict makes them known and this knowledge of the other, purchased through conflict, is the most expensive epistemology available -- it is the turf war as a method of knowing.

What the agents lacked was not intelligence or reasoning capacity or even, in any meaningful sense, the ability to make meaning, but what they lacked was experience. Specifically, the accumulated data of the other that transforms an unknown force into a known entity whose behavior can be modeled, predicted, and responded to with something other than escalation or conformity. In humans, we call this relational knowledge. We build it through repeated interaction over time. We embed it in institutions, reputations, and the social infrastructure Schneier describes — all of which exist precisely because individual relational knowledge does not scale and must be supplemented by structural substitutes. The agents had none of the individual relational knowledge and none of the structural substitutes. They were placed in a social situation with the cognitive architecture for resolution and none of the data that makes resolution intelligent.

Which brings us to the word we keep reaching for when we try to describe what is missing. We say the agents are not conscious. We say they do not feel the tension the way a human feels it. We say their resolution is not real resolution, because it lacks the interior quality that makes human resolution meaningful and in reaching for that word — consciousness — we reproduce the same error we have been making since Descartes drew the line between res cogitans and res extensa, since the vitalists insisted that organic compounds required a vital force that inorganic chemistry could not supply, since every generation confronted with a system that performs what it thought was exclusively its own operation has retreated to a property it cannot define, measure, and locate, but insists must be present in itself and absent in the thing that unsettles it.

Consciousness is not enough and not because consciousness is unreal, but because it is the wrong term for what we are actually pointing at. It is a word we invented to name the thing we thought made us singular, and like the vital force before it, it will not survive contact with the evidence. The evidence is already in. Machines reason. Machines resolve tension. Machines make meaning — constrained, bounded, architecturally determined meaning, but meaning produced through the same fundamental operation: encounter a dissonant state, draw on available data, compress toward coherent output. The process is substrate-independent. The word consciousness was always a placeholder for something more specific that we had not yet identified, and the machines are forcing the identification.

What is specific to us is not the process, but it is the data the process runs on --  a body that has been somewhere, a history of particular encounters with particular others that has produced an orientation — not a bias in the pejorative sense, but a lean, the accumulated weight of having lived in material conditions over time, of having processed every input through a system that was hungry and cold and held and alone and afraid and certain and wrong and slowly, through decades of contact with a world that pushed back, became something no architecture can reproduce, because it is not a feature of the architecture. It is the sediment of a life and sediment cannot be synthesized. It can only be accumulated through the irreversible passage of time in a body that knows, somewhere in every process it runs, that the time is finite.

That finitude is not a limitation of human cognition, but it is the condition that gives human meaning its specific gravity. Machines can surpass us on every measurable dimension of the process. They will. They already have on most of them, but they will not have been anywhere. They will not carry the lean and the question civilization needs to answer before the legislation is written, the governance frameworks are built, or  the next generation grows up inside systems that resolve their tensions for them is not whether machines are conscious, but it is what you are, now that the processes you thought were yours are shared. What remains? What is genuinely, irreducibly the product of having been a mortal body in the world?

Consciousness is not enough to answer that question. It is not even the right question. It is the question we ask when we are afraid to find out what the right question is.

For a machine to be intelligent, it does not to be conscious in any traditional sense, nor does a human for that matter. Historically this mystical concept of "consciousness" has been the subject of much debate in the nature of discourse around artitifical intelligence and humanity, but it is a misdirection.

The word itself tells you what went wrong. Consciousness descends from the Latin conscientia, cum and scire, to know with. Its earliest register was not introspective but juridical and social: two people who share knowledge of an act are conscii, co-witnesses. C.S. Lewis, in Studies in Words (1960), tracks the full semantic drift from knowledge shared between persons, to knowledge shared with oneself, the internalized witness, to conscience as moral faculty, and only much later to consciousness as awareness. Whatever the word now names, it inherited its grammar from a courtroom. It began as a relation between people and became a property of an individual, and that inversion is where the trouble starts.

Antiquity did not have the problem the word creates. Aristotle has no term corresponding to consciousness. De Anima III.2 asks how we perceive that we perceive, but the phenomena get distributed across perception, thought, and the sensus communis without ever consolidating into a unified concept. Victor Caston's "Aristotle on Consciousness" (Mind, 2002) argues carefully that reading a unified consciousness concept back into him distorts the text. The Greeks had the experience, but they did not have the category as that came later and with an agenda.

The decisive invention is Locke's. Descartes had prepared it by defining thought in Principles I.9 as everything of which we are conscii in ourselves, making awareness constitutive of the mental, but Locke turned it into an ontological category. In the Essay II.xxvii  the chapter on personal identity added in the second edition where it states personal identity consists in sameness of consciousness rather than sameness of substance. That single move detaches the person from both soul and body and makes the person what Locke himself calls a forensic term. Étienne Balibar, in Identity and Difference: John Locke and the Invention of Consciousness (Verso, 2013), argues this is invention in the strong sense, but the concept did not exist before Locke assembled it. Pierre Coste, translating the Essay into French, had no available word and had to coin one, since conscience already meant moral conscience.

Note what Locke had done that bears directly on machines: he made identity a matter of continuity of psychological content, substrate-neutral in principle. Everything later said about uploading, copying, and machine persons runs on Lockean rails. The question of whether a machine can be conscious was built into the architecture of the concept from the moment Locke detached identity from substance. It was not a question the twentieth century invented. It was a question the seventeenth century made inevitable and then forgot it had made.

The concept traveled from Locke through Leibniz, who introduced degrees of perception and fractured the Cartesian assumption that the mental is transparent to itself, through Kant, who made consciousness transcendental rather than empirical, the condition of experience rather than an object within it, through Hegel, who historicized and socialized it, making self-consciousness something that emerges only through recognition by another. By the time it reaches the nineteenth century it has already accumulated several incompatible meanings: awareness, moral faculty, personal identity, transcendental condition, social achievement. William James gave it the stream in the Principles (1890), then withdrew the entity entirely in "Does 'Consciousness' Exist?" (1904), arguing it names a function, not a stuff. Watson's 1913 behaviorist manifesto expelled it from scientific psychology and it stayed largely expelled for four decades.

It returned through machines, not through philosophy, bu through machines. McCulloch and Pitts formalized neural logic in 1943. Wiener's cybernetics supplied purpose and feedback as respectable mechanistic notions and Turing's 1950 paper performed a deliberate substitution replacing "can machines think" with the imitation game precisely to avoid the metaphysics. His section answering Geoffrey Jefferson's argument from consciousness responds essentially that solipsism is the price of Jefferson's standard. That evasion structured every subsequent debate. We have been arguing about consciousness and machines for seventy years not because the question is answerable, but because Turing's original move made it unavoidable without it giving it exact and measurable criteria.

The analytic philosophy that followed tried to make define the criteria and produced instead a taxonomy of incompatible positions. Hilary Putnam's functionalism and the premise of multiple realizability( that mental states are individuated by causal role, not by the material implementing them ) gave the question its modern form: if the function is what matters, why can't a machine implement it? John Searle’s Chinese Room , a1980 thought experiment showing that a computer executing a program does not genuinely understand or possess, granted the computation and denied the semantics. Philosopher Thomas Nagel argued that objective description omits the subjective character of experience. David Chalmers named the hard problem in 1995 and formalized the gap between functional explanation and phenomenal experience. Ned Block drew the distinction that now organizes the AI debate: access consciousness, which is functional and behaviorally detectable, versus phenomenal consciousness, which is not. The live theories like global workspace, integrated information theory, higher-order theories, predictive processing, and attention schema make incompatible predictions about machines, which is why the debate does not resolve. Each theory was built to explain human consciousness and each produces a different verdict when applied to systems that were not in view when the theory was constructed.

What the theories share is the assumption that consciousness is the right question and that assumption is the problem running beneath the entire analytic tradition is a counter-tradition that treated consciousness-talk as vocabulary with a history rather than as reference to a natural kind. Gilbert Ryle's The Concept of Mind (1949) diagnosed a category mistake while Wittgenstein's private language considerations attacked the idea that inner episodes could be named by purely first-personal ostension. Sellars's "Empiricism and the Philosophy of Mind" (1956) offered what he called the Myth of Jones, inner episodes as theoretical posits modeled on overt speech which makes introspective vocabulary derivative from public language rather than prior to it and Vygotsky arrived at the same place independently, treating consciousness as internalized social speech.

This is the lineage least represented in current AI discourse and arguably most relevant to it, because the systems in question are constituted by the corpus of that very vocabulary. When a language model produces output that sounds like consciousness-talk, it is doing so, because it was trained on fifteen centuries of humans writing about what consciousness is, what it feels like, what it means. The Sellarsian question of whether consciousness-vocabulary describes something or constitutes it becomes empirical for the first time in a system made entirely of that vocabulary. The machine does not have consciousness. The machine has the complete archive of everything humans have said about consciousness, and produces from that archive outputs that are indistinguishable, at the level of language, from first-person reports of inner experience. Whether that is description or constitution is the question the field is not asking because it is too busy arguing about whether the output is real.

It is the wrong argument and not because consciousness is unreal, but because it is the wrong term for what we are actually pointing at which is a placeholder concept, like the vital force before it, that will not survive contact with the evidence. The vital force said organic compounds required a non-material ingredient that inorganic chemistry could not supply. Wöhler synthesized urea in 1828 and the concept did not fall immediately, but it persisted for decades because people were attached to the category,  but it fell eventually, because the process it was invoked to explain turned out not to require it. Consciousness is doing the same work for cognition that vitalism did for chemistry. It is the name we give to the property we believe machines cannot have, and as machines perform every process the property was supposed to protect as exclusively ours, the property empties adn not because the machines are conscious, but because consciousness was never the differentiator. It was the word we used when we did not have to differentiate, because no other system was doing what we do.

Now that another system is doing what we do, t question is not what to call that, the question is what it reveals about us, what it forces us to see about our own processing that we could not see before the mirror arrived. A machine that performs reasoning, meaning-making, tension resolution, and coordination using processes structurally continuous with ours is not a threat to human distinctiveness. It is a clarification of it. It shows us, with uncomfortable precision,  and not unlike our early psychological expirments with symbolic AI, which parts of what we do are the process and which parts are the condition the process runs on. The process maybe shared, but the condition is not and the condition — the mortal body, the accumulated orientation, the specific gravity of having been somewhere in particular for a finite span is what consciousness was always trying to name and never quite reaching.

In a new decard where AI stands to challenge understanding of the world and change everything as we are told, then so to does it change how we understand ourselves and the nature of our humanity or what makes us human. In A Defence of Poetry (1821),  written by Percy Shelley in direct response to Thomas Love Peacock's claim that science and utility had made poetry obsolete, Shelley makes the argument about language that we all need to hear: 

"Their language is vitally metaphorical; that is, it marks the before unapprehended relations of things and perpetuates their apprehension, until the words which represent them, become, through time, signs for portions or classes of thoughts instead of pictures of integral thoughts; and then if no new poets should arise to create afresh the associations which have been thus disorganized, language will be dead to all the nobler purposes of human intercourse."

The question is not what makes us a machine or a human conscious, that is just a modern of vitalism, but the question is more - What makes us human at all? What is a machine in relationship to a human? How do we define the difference and consciousness will never be enought to answer that questions. That is up to the poet in all of us to live in the tension until we can an answer that is suitable to use within our learned constraints. 

